1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,861
Take a single electron. Now imagine you could cut it in half.

2
00:00:04,267 --> 00:00:12,041
Not physically, of course. But mathematically, every electron
can be written as two halves: two strange objects called

3
00:00:12,041 --> 00:00:13,227
Majorana fermions.

4
00:00:13,667 --> 00:00:22,119
Normally the two halves are glued together, sitting on the same
atom. But in the year 2000, Alexei Kitaev wrote down a simple

5
00:00:22,119 --> 00:00:25,229
model of a wire where the halves come unstuck,

6
00:00:25,667 --> 00:00:29,848
and end up at opposite ends of the wire, as far apart as you
like.

7
00:00:30,267 --> 00:00:38,395
One electron's worth of quantum information, stored nowhere in
particular. And that makes it remarkably hard to destroy.

8
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:41,851
This is the story of one short, beautiful paper.

9
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:50,840
To see why this matters, start with the problem Kitaev wanted to
solve: quantum memory.

10
00:00:51,266 --> 00:00:58,136
A qubit can be zero, one, or any superposition of the two. And
it is fragile in two distinct ways.

11
00:00:58,533 --> 00:01:02,032
A classical error, σˣ, flips zero into one.

12
00:01:02,466 --> 00:01:11,529
A phase error, σᶻ, leaves the bits alone, but flips the sign of
the one part relative to the zero part, quietly scrambling the

13
00:01:11,529 --> 00:01:12,536
superposition.

14
00:01:12,933 --> 00:01:21,807
Error-correcting codes can fix both, but only if every gate
fails less than about once in ten thousand tries. In 2000, that

15
00:01:21,807 --> 00:01:23,322
was far out of reach.

16
00:01:23,733 --> 00:01:27,488
So Kitaev asked: can the physics itself do the protecting?

17
00:01:27,933 --> 00:01:34,418
Here is a first idea. Let each qubit be a site that is either
empty, or holds one electron.

18
00:01:34,866 --> 00:01:43,231
Now a bit flip would mean creating or destroying an electron out
of nothing. Charge conservation forbids that. Even in a

19
00:01:43,231 --> 00:01:49,992
superconductor, where charge is only conserved modulo two, the
parity, even or odd, is protected.

20
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:57,643
The only loophole is an electron hopping from one site to
another, and that can be suppressed by putting the sites far

21
00:01:57,643 --> 00:01:59,914
apart, with an energy gap in between.

22
00:02:00,333 --> 00:02:08,768
But phase errors survive. Empty and full sites have different
energies, so they pick up different phases. The operator a†a,

23
00:02:08,768 --> 00:02:14,392
asking "is this site full?", is exactly a phase error, and
nothing local stops it.

24
00:02:15,666 --> 00:02:23,303
Here comes the mathematical trick. Each site has an annihilation
operator a, and a creation operator a†.

25
00:02:23,732 --> 00:02:34,277
Just as a complex number splits into a real and an imaginary
part, we can split a in two. c₁ equals a plus a†. And c₂ equals

26
00:02:34,277 --> 00:02:35,892
a minus a†, over i.

27
00:02:36,332 --> 00:02:45,014
These are Majorana operators. Each one is its own antiparticle,
c dagger equals c. Each squares to one, and different ones

28
00:02:45,014 --> 00:02:45,868
anticommute.

29
00:02:46,266 --> 00:02:53,370
Going backwards, a is c₁ plus i c₂, over two. Two Majoranas make
one ordinary fermion.

30
00:02:53,799 --> 00:03:02,162
Now rewrite the dangerous phase error, a†a. In Majoranas, it is
one half, times one plus i c₁ c₂.

31
00:03:02,599 --> 00:03:10,934
It needs both halves at once. If c₁ and c₂ lived on different,
distant sites, this error would require them to interact, and

32
00:03:10,934 --> 00:03:12,412
that could be avoided.

33
00:03:12,799 --> 00:03:21,115
And a single Majorana can never appear alone in any physical
Hamiltonian, because it would change the fermion parity. An

34
00:03:21,115 --> 00:03:25,066
isolated Majorana is immune to every kind of local error.

35
00:03:25,466 --> 00:03:33,972
The catch: Majoranas are not lying around in solids. The goal of
the paper is to build a Hamiltonian where they emerge as low

36
00:03:33,972 --> 00:03:40,164
energy degrees of freedom, and surprisingly, ordinary
non-interacting electrons are enough.

37
00:03:40,599 --> 00:03:49,977
The organizing idea is pairing. Any quadratic Hamiltonian can be
written as i over four, times a sum of A l m, c l c m. Its

38
00:03:49,977 --> 00:03:57,068
ground state pairs the Majoranas up. Usually all of them. But
sometimes, a few are left over.

39
00:03:58,866 --> 00:04:07,079
Kitaev's toy model is a chain of L sites, a quantum wire, lying
on the surface of a three-dimensional superconductor.

40
00:04:07,466 --> 00:04:16,359
Two ingredients are needed. First, superconductivity. The
symmetry behind charge conservation, U one, must be broken down

41
00:04:16,359 --> 00:04:24,298
to a simple sign flip, Z two. A Majorana mixes a with a dagger,
so only a Z two symmetry can leave it alone.

42
00:04:24,732 --> 00:04:32,531
Second, only one spin direction should be present. Think of
spinless electrons. In a footnote, Kitaev notes that this takes

43
00:04:32,531 --> 00:04:36,146
triplet, p-wave pairing in the superconductor underneath.

44
00:04:36,532 --> 00:04:43,338
The Hamiltonian has three terms. The hopping, w, lets an
electron jump between neighbouring sites.

45
00:04:43,732 --> 00:04:47,871
The chemical potential, mu, sets the energy cost of filling a
site.

46
00:04:48,266 --> 00:04:56,313
And the superconducting pairing, delta, creates or destroys
pairs of electrons on neighbouring sites. This is the term that

47
00:04:56,313 --> 00:04:58,079
breaks charge conservation.

48
00:04:58,466 --> 00:05:04,311
Now rewrite everything in Majoranas. Every site splits into a
blue half and a gold half.

49
00:05:04,732 --> 00:05:12,905
The chemical potential couples the two halves of the same site.
Hopping and pairing combine into bonds between neighbours. w

50
00:05:12,905 --> 00:05:19,495
plus delta couples each gold to the next blue, and delta minus w
couples each blue to the next gold.

51
00:05:19,932 --> 00:05:22,215
Now, let's look at two special cases.

52
00:05:23,398 --> 00:05:30,416
Case one. No hopping, no pairing, and a negative chemical
potential. Only the on-site term survives.

53
00:05:30,864 --> 00:05:39,149
So each blue Majorana pairs with the gold partner on its own
site. Every site is simply empty. It's boring, and completely

54
00:05:39,149 --> 00:05:39,760
ordinary.

55
00:05:40,198 --> 00:05:48,313
Case two. Set mu to zero, and make the hopping equal to the
pairing. Now the on-site term vanishes, and so does the bond

56
00:05:48,313 --> 00:05:50,139
from blue to the next gold.

57
00:05:50,531 --> 00:06:01,070
What's left is H equals i w, times the sum of c₂ⱼ c₂ⱼ₊₁. Each
gold Majorana pairs with the blue one on the next site.

58
00:06:01,464 --> 00:06:05,070
The pairing has slid over by one. And now, look at the ends.

59
00:06:05,464 --> 00:06:13,272
The very first Majorana, c₁, and the very last one, c₂L, appear
nowhere in the Hamiltonian. They are unpaired.

60
00:06:13,664 --> 00:06:22,273
Each bonded pair forms a new fermion, a tilde j, stretched
across two sites and costing energy two w. The ground state

61
00:06:22,273 --> 00:06:24,096
leaves all of them empty.

62
00:06:24,531 --> 00:06:33,380
But the two leftovers, b prime and b double prime, form one more
fermion mode, and it costs zero energy. So there are two ground

63
00:06:33,380 --> 00:06:37,459
states, psi zero and psi one, with exactly the same energy.

64
00:06:37,864 --> 00:06:43,731
They differ in fermion parity. One has an even number of
electrons, the other an odd number.

65
00:06:44,131 --> 00:06:51,987
Here's the subtle part. In the bulk, the two phases look
identical. One is just a relabeling of the other, shifting every

66
00:06:51,987 --> 00:06:56,142
Majorana over by one. Only at the ends does the difference show.

67
00:06:56,531 --> 00:07:04,062
Or, as Kitaev puts it: you cannot cut a physical site in half.
If you could, both kinds of boundary would be possible.

68
00:07:05,664 --> 00:07:14,561
So here is our qubit. Empty or occupied, it lives in a single
fermion mode built from two Majoranas at opposite ends of the

69
00:07:14,561 --> 00:07:14,922
wire.

70
00:07:15,330 --> 00:07:24,170
Try to read it locally. Noise near the left end can only touch b
prime. But b prime alone changes parity, so it cannot appear in

71
00:07:24,170 --> 00:07:31,629
any Hamiltonian. To learn anything, you need b prime times b
double prime. You must touch both ends at once.

72
00:07:32,064 --> 00:07:40,409
A bit flip needs a whole electron to appear or vanish. Parity
forbids it. A phase error needs the two ends to talk. Distance

73
00:07:40,409 --> 00:07:42,496
and the energy gap suppress it.

74
00:07:42,930 --> 00:07:51,283
Both kinds of error are blocked, not by clever correction, but
by geometry. This is what the abstract means by a qubit

75
00:07:51,283 --> 00:07:53,832
intrinsically immune to decoherence.

76
00:07:54,264 --> 00:08:03,010
But fine-tuned special points rarely survive in the real world.
What happens away from w equals delta, and mu equals zero?

77
00:08:04,663 --> 00:08:13,690
To find out, look at the bulk. In an infinite wire, an
excitation with momentum q costs energy epsilon of q: the square

78
00:08:13,690 --> 00:08:19,682
root of, two w cos q plus mu, squared, plus four delta squared,
sine squared q.

79
00:08:20,130 --> 00:08:26,615
Start in the case we just solved. There is a healthy gap: no
excitations anywhere near zero energy.

80
00:08:27,063 --> 00:08:29,069
Now raise mu. The gap shrinks,

81
00:08:29,463 --> 00:08:32,493
and closes exactly when mu reaches two w.

82
00:08:33,063 --> 00:08:37,245
Past that point, it opens again, but we are now in the trivial
phase.

83
00:08:37,663 --> 00:08:45,535
There is no way from one phase to the other without closing the
gap. That is the hallmark of a topological phase transition.

84
00:08:45,930 --> 00:08:54,995
Mapping it out: the phase with unpaired Majoranas fills the
whole region where two w exceeds mu in size, as long as delta is

85
00:08:54,995 --> 00:08:58,431
not zero. Everywhere else, the wire is trivial.

86
00:08:58,863 --> 00:09:07,282
And along the line delta equals zero, inside that region, the
wire is just a normal metal, with no gap at all. Pairing,

87
00:09:07,282 --> 00:09:09,829
however weak, is what opens the gap.

88
00:09:11,063 --> 00:09:19,439
Are the end Majoranas still there away from the sweet spot?
Kitaev looks for zero energy modes built from powers of two

89
00:09:19,439 --> 00:09:21,410
numbers, x plus and x minus.

90
00:09:21,797 --> 00:09:29,832
In the trivial region, one root is larger than one, and the
other smaller. A mode that decays from an edge cannot then

91
00:09:29,832 --> 00:09:33,850
satisfy the boundary condition. So there are no zero modes.

92
00:09:34,263 --> 00:09:42,837
In the topological region, both roots are smaller than one. b
prime decays away from the left end, and b double prime from the

93
00:09:42,837 --> 00:09:43,245
right.

94
00:09:43,663 --> 00:09:52,134
Here are the real modes, found by diagonalizing a forty site
chain. As mu creeps toward two w, they spread deeper into the

95
00:09:52,134 --> 00:09:54,842
wire, but they stay pinned to the ends.

96
00:09:55,263 --> 00:10:04,680
In a finite wire, the two tails overlap ever so slightly. That
gives a tiny effective coupling: H effective equals i over two,

97
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:06,698
t, b prime, b double prime.

98
00:10:07,130 --> 00:10:16,331
And t falls off exponentially with the length of the wire: t is
proportional to e to the minus L over l zero. On a logarithmic

99
00:10:16,331 --> 00:10:18,010
scale, a straight line.

100
00:10:18,397 --> 00:10:26,417
Make the wire a few correlation lengths longer, and the
splitting drops by orders of magnitude. This even survives weak

101
00:10:26,417 --> 00:10:33,223
interactions: t is just the amplitude for a quasiparticle to
tunnel the full length of a gapped wire.

102
00:10:34,530 --> 00:10:42,799
Now Kitaev asks a deeper question. Forget the toy model. Given
any gapped one-dimensional superconductor, how can you tell

103
00:10:42,799 --> 00:10:45,239
whether it hosts unpaired Majoranas?

104
00:10:45,663 --> 00:10:54,840
Define the Majorana number, M. It is minus one if there are
unpaired Majoranas, and plus one if not. Two parallel chains can

105
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:58,762
pair their end Majoranas and cancel, so M multiplies.

106
00:10:59,197 --> 00:11:06,301
Remarkably, you don't even need ends. Close the chain into a
loop, and look at the parity of its unique ground state.

107
00:11:06,730 --> 00:11:13,130
Take two loops, of lengths L₁ and L₂. Cut them open, and
reconnect them into one big loop.

108
00:11:13,530 --> 00:11:22,340
Wherever two ends meet, a coupling u fixes the sign of i b
double prime b prime. Reconnecting swaps which end meets which,

109
00:11:22,340 --> 00:11:25,157
and that costs exactly one factor of M.

110
00:11:25,597 --> 00:11:33,685
So M is written into the bulk itself. For free fermions, the
ground state parity is the sign of a Pfaffian: a square root of

111
00:11:33,685 --> 00:11:38,055
a determinant, which remembers the sign that a determinant
forgets.

112
00:11:38,463 --> 00:11:47,563
Fourier transform the loop. Every momentum q pairs up with minus
q, and each pair contributes a positive determinant. Only q

113
00:11:47,563 --> 00:11:51,306
equals zero and q equals pi are their own partners.

114
00:11:51,730 --> 00:12:00,494
The result: M equals the sign of the Pfaffian at zero, times the
sign of the Pfaffian at pi. A topological invariant, read off

115
00:12:00,494 --> 00:12:03,485
from just two points in the Brillouin zone.

116
00:12:03,930 --> 00:12:14,286
For our chain, the Pfaffian at zero is minus mu minus two w. At
pi, it is minus mu plus two w. Their product is negative exactly

117
00:12:14,286 --> 00:12:18,735
when two w beats mu. The phase diagram falls right out.

118
00:12:19,997 --> 00:12:27,847
When superconductivity is weak, this becomes beautifully simple.
Turn off delta and look at the ordinary electron band.

119
00:12:28,263 --> 00:12:37,181
M equals minus one, to the power nu of pi minus nu of zero. And
that is just the number of Fermi points between zero and pi.

120
00:12:37,597 --> 00:12:46,002
So count them. One Fermi point in each half of the Brillouin
zone: odd. Add any small gap, and you get unpaired Majoranas.

121
00:12:46,397 --> 00:12:51,666
Lower the band until it no longer crosses: zero Fermi points,
even, trivial.

122
00:12:52,063 --> 00:13:00,703
And here is why real materials make this hard. Electrons have
spin. Spin up and spin down usually give identical bands, so

123
00:13:00,703 --> 00:13:04,031
every Fermi point comes in a pair. Always even.

124
00:13:04,463 --> 00:13:13,326
Each spin component counts separately. To get an odd number, the
spin degeneracy must be lifted, which needs broken time reversal

125
00:13:13,326 --> 00:13:16,623
symmetry. Spin-orbit coupling alone will not do.

126
00:13:17,063 --> 00:13:25,969
Kitaev speculates that a magnetic field plus a charge density
wave might gap out just one spin species, leaving an odd count

127
00:13:25,969 --> 00:13:26,471
behind.

128
00:13:27,663 --> 00:13:33,530
How could anyone actually see these Majoranas? The paper ends
with a striking proposal.

129
00:13:33,930 --> 00:13:42,424
Remember the superconducting phase, theta. The Majorana
operators carry a factor e to the i theta over two. Wind theta

130
00:13:42,424 --> 00:13:45,663
by two pi, and every Majorana flips its sign.

131
00:13:46,063 --> 00:13:54,333
The Hamiltonian comes back to itself. The ground states don't,
quite. The winding applies the operator V, minus i b prime b

132
00:13:54,333 --> 00:14:00,719
double prime, which is equivalent to moving an electron from one
end of the chain to the other.

133
00:14:01,130 --> 00:14:08,405
Now build a Josephson junction out of the wire: two
superconductors, with a phase difference theta between them.

134
00:14:08,797 --> 00:14:17,952
Advance theta by two pi, and a single fermionic quasiparticle is
carried to the junction. The Majoranas switch from psi zero to

135
00:14:17,952 --> 00:14:21,341
psi one. The system is now on the other branch.

136
00:14:21,730 --> 00:14:30,875
So the Josephson energy is two-valued, and the current is four
pi periodic, instead of the usual two pi. A fingerprint of

137
00:14:30,875 --> 00:14:32,311
unpaired Majoranas.

138
00:14:32,730 --> 00:14:41,276
For a finite wire, the two branches almost cross at theta equals
pi, split only by the tiny t. And the paper even sketches how

139
00:14:41,276 --> 00:14:47,109
phase slips on a small island might be switched on and off to
implement quantum gates.

140
00:14:48,397 --> 00:14:55,522
That's the whole paper: sixteen pages, and a model its author
called rather unrealistic. It launched a field.

141
00:14:55,930 --> 00:15:04,442
A decade later, theorists showed how semiconductor nanowires,
with strong spin-orbit coupling, a magnetic field, and a nearby

142
00:15:04,442 --> 00:15:13,022
superconductor, could realize Kitaev's chain. Experiments ever
since have hunted for its zero energy signatures, with exciting

143
00:15:13,022 --> 00:15:14,725
hints, and lively debate.

144
00:15:15,130 --> 00:15:21,957
Majorana based topological qubits remain one of the most
ambitious routes to a quantum computer.

145
00:15:22,397 --> 00:15:26,407
So, to recap. Split every electron into two Majoranas.

146
00:15:26,797 --> 00:15:29,506
Pair them across sites, instead of within them.

147
00:15:29,930 --> 00:15:35,391
Two halves are left stranded at the ends, sharing one qubit that
no local noise can touch.

148
00:15:35,797 --> 00:15:42,367
And whether a wire can do this is decided by a single sign, the
Majorana number, hidden in its bulk.

149
00:15:42,797 --> 00:15:48,621
Protection, not from engineering, but from topology. That is the
beauty of Kitaev's idea.
